  "findings": [
    "The checkpoint options receive the same actual exclusive_guard.custody before connect. This covers the separately opened checkpoint connection; the two earlier pool bindings remain unchanged.",
    "Exact checkpoint SQL, close await, error precedence and the Memory recovery authority/validation/publication/ACK body are byte-preserved outside the specified argument replacement.",
    "The new owned child is a normal libtest entry. Its exact SQLITE_TRACE_STMT arms progress on the same database; a live Tokio runtime drives actual Memory recovery until the native callback is held.",
    "Cancellation drops only the actual recovery future. The parent independently opens the real fence file and probes it, then requires callback gate_status=0, so callback timeout cannot establish the negative.",
    "The positive requires callback release, target TRACE_CLOSE, independent physical lock availability in both child and parent, gate_status=1, and a successful child reap. TRACE_CLOSE is not treated as sqlite3_close return.",
    "The negative removes only this checkpoint native token attachment, retaining the same incoming owner as a normal future local and preserving both pool bindings. Its valid endpoint requires physical lock acquisition while callback status remains zero.",
    "Parent cleanup closes channels, kills and waits the owned direct child, and joins its stdout reader; no descendant process is created by this control. Non-marker stdout is discarded rather than preserved as a complete child log.",
    "No source changes were required by this review. The original recovery test file is byte-preserved except for its new module declaration."
  ],
